<h1>Well Pump Replacement: Cost, Signs, and Lifespan</h1>
<p>A well pump replacement is the single biggest routine expense of owning a well — and the most predictable. Submersible pumps in Central Florida typically last 10 to 15 years, and the average replacement lands around $1,900, covering the pump, labor to pull and reset it, and basic electrical and disinfection work.</p>
<p>The mistake to avoid is replacing blind. A failing pressure tank can mimic a dying pump, and a $150 pressure switch can mimic both. A proper quote starts with diagnosis, then itemizes pump, tank, and controls separately so you only buy what actually failed.</p>
<div class="card"><h2>What we handle</h2><ul><li>Cost drivers: depth, horsepower, tank condition</li>
<li>Failure signs: cycling, pressure loss, air, sand, breaker trips</li>
<li>Jet vs submersible — which your well uses</li>
<li>Questions to ask before approving any quote</li></ul></div>
<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>What is the average well pump replacement cost?</h3><p>Around $1,900 in Central Florida for a standard submersible pump. Shallow jet pumps cost less; deep, high-horsepower, or tank-included jobs cost more.</p>
<h3>How long should a well pump last?</h3><p>Plan on 10–15 years. Frequent cycling, a waterlogged tank, and heavy irrigation use are the top lifespan killers — all fixable once spotted.</p>
